<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anne Robert Jacques Turgot</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1727">1727</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Turgot, Baron de l'Aulne</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anne Robert Jacques Turgot, Baron de l'Aulne, was a French economist and statesman, sometimes considered a physiocrat and best remembered as an early advocate of economic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>. As intendant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limoges">Limoges</a> in 1761–1774 he sought a fairer assessment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taille">taille</a> and replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> with a money tax, and in 1766 he wrote his Reflections on the Formation and Distribution of Wealth. Appointed Controller-General of Finance by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xvi">Louis XVI</a> in 1774, he freed the grain trade and in 1776 presented the Six Edicts, which suppressed the corvées and the privileges of the craft <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>. Opposition at court forced his resignation in May 1776, and he spent his last years in scientific and literary study.</p></div>
